WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.245
Lesson 6.1.

00:00:02.245 --> 00:00:04.000
Protocol due diligence.

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:07.710
Today you'll run the six-step research loop from Lesson

00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:10.294
6.0 in real depth, one step at a time,

00:00:10.294 --> 00:00:13.010
and land it on an actual written verdict.

00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:17.624
Here's the objective for this lesson, in one sentence.

00:00:17.624 --> 00:00:20.195
Run the six-step research loop on a real

00:00:20.195 --> 00:00:23.017
protocol, and reach a written verdict: not a

00:00:23.017 --> 00:00:26.027
vague feeling about it, an actual verdict, with

00:00:26.027 --> 00:00:28.849
a size, conditions, and an exit written down.

00:00:30.149 --> 00:00:32.300
Here's the loop from last lesson again,

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:34.804
since this entire video walks through it.

00:00:34.804 --> 00:00:39.279
Mechanism, cash flow, dependencies, solvency, evidence, and exit.

00:00:39.279 --> 00:00:42.409
Six questions, asked in order, every single time.

00:00:43.709 --> 00:00:45.244
Step one: mechanism.

00:00:46.544 --> 00:00:49.831
Mechanism means answering three concrete questions

00:00:49.831 --> 00:00:52.258
about a protocol's own contracts.

00:00:52.258 --> 00:00:57.227
Who actually uses this: lenders, borrowers, traders, liquidity providers.

00:00:57.227 --> 00:00:59.340
What assets actually move through it:

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:01.252
which tokens, on which chain.

00:01:01.252 --> 00:01:04.582
And what incentive actually keeps people using it:

00:01:04.582 --> 00:01:08.107
real yield, token rewards, or something else entirely.

00:01:09.408 --> 00:01:12.181
Worth being direct here: mechanism is about

00:01:12.181 --> 00:01:14.828
what the contracts genuinely do, not what

00:01:14.828 --> 00:01:16.703
the website says they do.

00:01:16.703 --> 00:01:19.635
A protocol's own marketing page and its actual

00:01:19.635 --> 00:01:23.129
deployed contracts aren't always the same document, and

00:01:23.129 --> 00:01:24.938
this step is where you check.

00:01:26.238 --> 00:01:27.638
Step two: cash flow.

00:01:28.938 --> 00:01:31.063
Cash flow means separating two very

00:01:31.063 --> 00:01:33.548
different sources of protocol income.

00:01:33.548 --> 00:01:36.688
Real revenue: actual fees and interest, paid

00:01:36.688 --> 00:01:39.710
by actual users, for an actual service.

00:01:39.710 --> 00:01:43.084
And emissions: newly minted tokens, handed out as

00:01:43.084 --> 00:01:46.661
rewards, that dilute existing holders whether or not

00:01:46.661 --> 00:01:49.630
the protocol itself is genuinely profitable.

00:01:50.929 --> 00:01:54.279
Here's why that distinction actually matters, using

00:01:54.279 --> 00:01:57.284
a purely illustrative example: Protocol Z.

00:01:57.284 --> 00:01:59.778
It pays out five hundred thousand dollars a

00:01:59.778 --> 00:02:02.725
month in reward emissions, but collects only eighty

00:02:02.725 --> 00:02:05.179
thousand dollars a month in real fees.

00:02:05.179 --> 00:02:07.627
That's real revenue covering just sixteen

00:02:07.627 --> 00:02:09.559
percent of what it pays out.

00:02:09.559 --> 00:02:12.797
With a seven-million-dollar rewards budget left in its

00:02:12.797 --> 00:02:15.858
treasury, that gap runs for roughly fourteen months

00:02:15.858 --> 00:02:18.389
before the rewards have to shrink, or stop.

00:02:19.689 --> 00:02:23.232
That's the actual risk emissions-funded activity carries.

00:02:23.232 --> 00:02:26.304
It's not that emissions are inherently bad; new

00:02:26.304 --> 00:02:29.804
protocols often need them to bootstrap real usage.

00:02:29.804 --> 00:02:32.779
It's that emissions are temporary by construction,

00:02:32.779 --> 00:02:34.704
and this step is where you check

00:02:34.704 --> 00:02:37.212
whether real revenue is growing to replace

00:02:37.212 --> 00:02:39.604
them before the budget actually runs out.

00:02:40.904 --> 00:02:42.499
Step three: dependencies.

00:02:43.799 --> 00:02:46.468
This step is exactly Module five's own

00:02:46.468 --> 00:02:50.189
dependency map, run against one specific protocol.

00:02:50.189 --> 00:02:52.415
Oracles: what feeds it prices, and

00:02:52.415 --> 00:02:54.559
how manipulable is that feed.

00:02:54.559 --> 00:02:56.756
Bridges: does it hold or move assets

00:02:56.756 --> 00:02:59.549
across chains, and under what trust model.

00:02:59.549 --> 00:03:02.664
Admin keys: who can upgrade it, and how fast.

00:03:02.664 --> 00:03:05.067
Stablecoins: which ones does it accept as

00:03:05.067 --> 00:03:07.769
collateral, and are they genuinely backed.

00:03:07.769 --> 00:03:09.880
And other protocols: what does it build

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:11.674
on top of, that could itself fail.

00:03:12.974 --> 00:03:16.267
If Module five's dependency map felt abstract at the

00:03:16.267 --> 00:03:19.797
time, this is exactly where it stops being abstract.

00:03:19.797 --> 00:03:22.359
Every one of those five links is a real,

00:03:22.359 --> 00:03:25.795
checkable fact about one specific protocol in front of

00:03:25.795 --> 00:03:28.857
you right now, not a general concept to remember.

00:03:30.157 --> 00:03:31.752
Step four: solvency.

00:03:33.052 --> 00:03:35.968
Solvency means checking three things about how a

00:03:35.968 --> 00:03:39.005
protocol handles the money it currently holds.

00:03:39.005 --> 00:03:43.210
Collateral quality: is what's backing deposits genuinely liquid

00:03:43.210 --> 00:03:47.255
and independently priced, or thin and easily manipulated.

00:03:47.255 --> 00:03:50.345
Liquidation design: does it actually liquidate bad

00:03:50.345 --> 00:03:53.310
positions fast enough, before losses spread.

00:03:53.310 --> 00:03:56.497
And bad-debt paths: if a liquidation genuinely

00:03:56.497 --> 00:03:58.735
fails, who absorbs that loss — a

00:03:58.735 --> 00:04:01.515
reserve fund, or every depositor at once.

00:04:02.815 --> 00:04:04.963
Solvency problems are quiet almost

00:04:04.963 --> 00:04:06.920
right up until they aren't.

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:10.000
A protocol can carry real, satisfied users for

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:13.212
months on thin collateral and a slow liquidation

00:04:13.212 --> 00:04:16.226
design, and the failure, when it comes, tends

00:04:16.226 --> 00:04:19.110
to arrive all at once rather than gradually.

00:04:20.410 --> 00:04:21.875
Step five: evidence.

00:04:23.175 --> 00:04:25.055
This step means checking four

00:04:25.055 --> 00:04:27.485
kinds of real, verifiable sources.

00:04:27.485 --> 00:04:31.049
Explorers: the actual on-chain contracts and transaction

00:04:31.049 --> 00:04:33.350
history, not a claim about them.

00:04:33.350 --> 00:04:35.644
Audits: who reviewed the code, when, and

00:04:35.644 --> 00:04:38.237
whether every finding was actually fixed.

00:04:38.237 --> 00:04:40.570
Governance: the real proposal and vote

00:04:40.570 --> 00:04:43.322
history, not just the current parameters.

00:04:43.322 --> 00:04:45.607
And independent data: numbers from a

00:04:45.607 --> 00:04:48.262
source the protocol itself doesn't control.

00:04:49.562 --> 00:04:52.505
Here's the actual standard this step holds you to:

00:04:52.505 --> 00:04:55.506
a claim only counts once you've genuinely traced it

00:04:55.506 --> 00:04:58.287
back to one of those four sources yourself.

00:04:58.287 --> 00:05:01.037
A protocol's own blog post, describing its

00:05:01.037 --> 00:05:03.127
own audit, is not the audit.

00:05:03.127 --> 00:05:04.842
Go find the actual report.

00:05:06.142 --> 00:05:07.537
Step six: exit.

00:05:08.837 --> 00:05:11.209
Exit means writing down the actual unwind,

00:05:11.209 --> 00:05:13.140
before you ever need it, not while

00:05:13.140 --> 00:05:15.977
you're already trying to leave under pressure.

00:05:15.977 --> 00:05:19.037
The exact steps to withdraw or close the position.

00:05:19.037 --> 00:05:20.954
The realistic gas cost of doing

00:05:20.954 --> 00:05:22.992
it, at a normal network load.

00:05:22.992 --> 00:05:24.996
The likely slippage if you had to

00:05:24.996 --> 00:05:26.887
exit the full size at once.

00:05:26.887 --> 00:05:30.137
And every token approval you'd need to revoke afterward.

00:05:31.437 --> 00:05:33.177
This is exactly the step this

00:05:33.177 --> 00:05:36.202
module's earlier lessons warned about skipping.

00:05:36.202 --> 00:05:38.739
An exit you've never actually mapped out is

00:05:38.739 --> 00:05:41.276
an exit you'll be improvising for the first

00:05:41.276 --> 00:05:43.812
time during whatever event made you want to

00:05:43.812 --> 00:05:46.061
leave in the first place, which is the

00:05:46.061 --> 00:05:48.367
worst possible moment to be improvising.

00:05:49.667 --> 00:05:51.822
Logging it: evidenced, not assumed.

00:05:53.122 --> 00:05:55.629
Here's the one rule that makes this loop

00:05:55.629 --> 00:05:58.503
actually worth running: log every step in your

00:05:58.503 --> 00:06:01.377
own due-diligence tracker, and tick a box only

00:06:01.377 --> 00:06:04.857
once it's genuinely evidenced, never merely assumed.

00:06:04.857 --> 00:06:08.167
An assumed box says you expect it's probably fine.

00:06:08.167 --> 00:06:10.044
An evidenced box says you actually

00:06:10.044 --> 00:06:11.707
checked, and here's the source.

00:06:13.007 --> 00:06:15.464
This single rule is what separates real

00:06:15.464 --> 00:06:18.467
due diligence from simply feeling reassured.

00:06:18.467 --> 00:06:21.175
Six ticked boxes built on assumptions is a

00:06:21.175 --> 00:06:24.497
false sense of safety with a checklist attached.

00:06:24.497 --> 00:06:26.930
Six ticked boxes built on evidence is

00:06:26.930 --> 00:06:29.427
an actual verdict you can stand behind.

00:06:30.727 --> 00:06:32.312
The worked example, in full.

00:06:33.612 --> 00:06:35.605
Here's how all six steps actually

00:06:35.605 --> 00:06:37.547
show up in one real verdict.

00:06:37.547 --> 00:06:41.632
Mechanism: Protocol Y, a lending market, on Arbitrum.

00:06:41.632 --> 00:06:44.326
Cash flow: fees are real, with two million

00:06:44.326 --> 00:06:47.132
dollars a year in genuine borrow demand.

00:06:47.132 --> 00:06:50.154
Dependencies: one collateral asset uses a

00:06:50.154 --> 00:06:52.972
thin-market oracle, a genuine risk.

00:06:52.972 --> 00:06:57.426
Governance found in dependencies: upgrades carry a twenty-four hour

00:06:57.426 --> 00:07:01.132
timelock, some real protection, but not a large one.

00:07:01.132 --> 00:07:02.662
Evidence: all four of that

00:07:02.662 --> 00:07:04.832
gathered and traced, not assumed.

00:07:04.832 --> 00:07:08.622
Exit: a stablecoins-only position keeps the exit simple.

00:07:09.922 --> 00:07:12.432
Read that whole verdict as one sentence, the

00:07:12.432 --> 00:07:15.187
way it actually gets written in the tracker.

00:07:15.187 --> 00:07:17.812
Protocol Y, lending, Arbitrum.

00:07:17.812 --> 00:07:19.247
Risk: medium.

00:07:19.247 --> 00:07:22.638
Fees are real, borrow demand two million dollars a

00:07:22.638 --> 00:07:26.361
year, but one collateral uses a thin-market oracle, and

00:07:26.361 --> 00:07:29.552
upgrades carry only a twenty-four hour timelock.

00:07:30.852 --> 00:07:32.055
Why the rating is the

00:07:32.055 --> 00:07:33.587
worst risk, not the average.

00:07:34.887 --> 00:07:36.870
Here's the mistake, made concrete

00:07:36.870 --> 00:07:39.212
with a purely illustrative example.

00:07:39.212 --> 00:07:41.449
Say four of your five checks come

00:07:41.449 --> 00:07:44.410
back genuinely strong: real cash flow, solid

00:07:44.410 --> 00:07:47.407
governance, a clean audit, a mapped exit.

00:07:47.407 --> 00:07:50.190
Averaging those five checks might suggest the

00:07:50.190 --> 00:07:53.092
protocol is roughly eighty percent healthy.

00:07:53.092 --> 00:07:55.399
But the fifth check found a thin-market

00:07:55.399 --> 00:07:58.122
oracle, a genuine single point of failure.

00:07:58.122 --> 00:08:01.223
The real rating is medium-to-high risk, not eighty

00:08:01.223 --> 00:08:04.324
percent healthy, because that one failure point is

00:08:04.324 --> 00:08:07.181
enough to lose the position, regardless of how

00:08:07.181 --> 00:08:08.762
strong the other four are.

00:08:10.062 --> 00:08:12.535
Here's why that's the actual correct way to

00:08:12.535 --> 00:08:15.027
rate it, not just a conservative habit.

00:08:15.027 --> 00:08:17.894
A chain is exactly as strong as its weakest

00:08:17.894 --> 00:08:21.021
link, and a protocol's risk works the same way:

00:08:21.021 --> 00:08:24.148
it fails at its single worst dependency, not at

00:08:24.148 --> 00:08:27.927
some blended average of all its dependencies put together.

00:08:29.227 --> 00:08:31.222
The verdict format, one more time.

00:08:32.522 --> 00:08:35.435
Break Protocol Y's own verdict down into its

00:08:35.435 --> 00:08:38.995
three real components, since every single verdict this

00:08:38.995 --> 00:08:42.272
module asks for follows this exact same shape.

00:08:42.272 --> 00:08:45.127
Size: supply stablecoins only, meaning the

00:08:45.127 --> 00:08:48.082
riskiest collateral is avoided entirely.

00:08:48.082 --> 00:08:50.697
Conditions: cap it at ten percent of the

00:08:50.697 --> 00:08:54.442
whole DeFi book, a specific, decided-in-advance limit.

00:08:54.442 --> 00:08:57.133
And exit: alert on any queued upgrade, a

00:08:57.133 --> 00:09:00.547
concrete trigger, decided now, not improvised later.

00:09:01.847 --> 00:09:02.937
Quick check.

00:09:02.937 --> 00:09:05.361
In the worked example, what specific exit

00:09:05.361 --> 00:09:07.497
trigger did the verdict actually set?

00:09:11.917 --> 00:09:14.242
The answer: alert on any queued upgrade.

00:09:15.542 --> 00:09:16.637
Before you move on.

00:09:17.937 --> 00:09:21.097
Confirm three things before calling this lesson done.

00:09:21.097 --> 00:09:23.068
All six steps written, with real

00:09:23.068 --> 00:09:25.637
sources for each one, not assumptions.

00:09:25.637 --> 00:09:28.688
The risk rating set to the single worst material

00:09:28.688 --> 00:09:32.102
risk found, never an average across all six steps.

00:09:32.102 --> 00:09:34.822
And the verdict itself states a size, real

00:09:34.822 --> 00:09:37.542
conditions, and an actual exit, every time.

00:09:38.842 --> 00:09:39.932
Quick check.

00:09:39.932 --> 00:09:42.256
Why is the rating set to the worst risk

00:09:42.256 --> 00:09:45.218
found, rather than an average across all six steps?

00:09:49.638 --> 00:09:51.915
The answer: one real failure point is

00:09:51.915 --> 00:09:54.251
enough to lose the position, no matter

00:09:54.251 --> 00:09:56.468
how strong the other five checks are.

00:09:57.768 --> 00:09:58.808
One more.

00:09:58.808 --> 00:10:01.800
Which of the six steps exists specifically to prevent

00:10:01.800 --> 00:10:04.238
getting stuck in a position you can't leave?

00:10:08.658 --> 00:10:10.563
The answer: step six, exit.

00:10:11.863 --> 00:10:14.068
Let's recap this entire lesson.

00:10:14.068 --> 00:10:16.684
The six-step loop, run in real depth:

00:10:16.684 --> 00:10:21.183
mechanism, cash flow, dependencies, solvency, evidence, exit.

00:10:21.183 --> 00:10:23.848
Log every step in your tracker, and tick a

00:10:23.848 --> 00:10:27.928
box only once it's genuinely evidenced, never merely assumed.

00:10:27.928 --> 00:10:29.604
Rate the risk at the single

00:10:29.604 --> 00:10:32.358
worst material finding, never an average.

00:10:32.358 --> 00:10:34.758
And every verdict ends the same way:

00:10:34.758 --> 00:10:37.288
a size, real conditions, and an actual

00:10:37.288 --> 00:10:39.753
exit, written down before you need it.

00:10:41.053 --> 00:10:43.888
Pick one real protocol, and write the full

00:10:43.888 --> 00:10:48.143
six-step verdict today, size, conditions, and exit included.

00:10:48.143 --> 00:10:50.923
Next up, Lesson 6.2: tokenomics

00:10:50.923 --> 00:10:53.268
— supply, unlocks, and FDV.
